    "md5": "bf6fc721c0c65a6c1723fd7bb69631d0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1780,
    "chunk_text": "When this Government took office, Sir, I actually wanted to wish the Government well. The reason for that is this. As you know, we have had basically two parties governing this country for the last 77 years; there had been changes in names and certain postures but, by and large, it had been those two parties or formations which had governed this country. When the Hon. President, as Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development, presented the Budget, he mentioned that; he also mentioned that in his Policy Statement. So, this Government's coming into power marks a radical departure from that 77-year history and whatever one may say about the JVP, which is the predominant constituent of the National People\u2019s Power, it has stood for certain principles.\n\nSo, when we look upon this Budget, the test, Sir, is whether there is a significant departure from the previous way of looking at the key issues facing the Tamil people of the North and the East - we represent the Tamil people of the North and the East - which had made us oppose the\n\nprevious governments. That is not rocket science; that is the key test we would apply when we look at this Budget. So, in that sense, I mentioned that we were hopeful and were actually looking forward to this Budget because there is an element within this Government, the predominant party, the JVP, being a leftist-oriented party, that it would not be too enamored by centre-right and right-wing economic policies, but there would be a tendency towards looking at the vulnerable, paying special attention to them, to protect them and their economies, making sure their economies would grow. That is a key element of leftist thinking. So, that was something we were actually looking forward to in this Government\u2019s Budget."
  },
