    "chunk_text": "\"Sri Lanka\u2019s 15 per cent revenue target in 2025, can be achieved and the deficit may be better than expected depending on the rollout of capital expenditure, but in the long term remains challenging, Fitch Rating has said.\n\n'However, it will depend heavily on a smooth liberalization of import restrictions, notably for vehicles.'\"\n\nWhen Fitch Ratings says this, your Deputy Minister says otherwise. He is already discouraging people from opening LCs to bring down vehicles, hinting that the duties may be reduced soon. So, how can you ever reach this Rs. 300 billion revenue target? That is the problem.\n\nNow, I would like to offer some advice, but, of course, it may not be looked upon well. Anyway, I have to responsibly critique this Budget, Sir.\n\nLet us take public pension system sustainability. We have to be really concerned about this. With the increase in public sector wages and pension adjustments, a serious concern arises over the strain on State resources in the medium to long term. This is what Fitch Ratings is talking about. Sri Lanka\u2019s public pension scheme is an unfunded scheme, meaning that pensions are paid directly from the Consolidated Fund, making it financially unsustainable as the ageing population increases and there is going to be a serious strain on the country\u2019s Treasury. Therefore, a sustainable pension reform strategy is required to transition towards a contributory pension system while ensuring the current beneficiaries are not affected. Sir, the Government must take this issue head-on and initiate a transition strategy. Gradual transition to a contributory pension scheme and introducing a hybrid pension system where new entrants to the public sector contribute to a pension fund while existing employees could have partially funded pensions is important. As far as public-private pension fund management is concerned, an independent pension fund managed with strict oversight and professional investment strategies must be"
