    "chunk_size_bytes": 1600,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Deputy Chairperson, in the 2025 Budget, for defence, there has been an allocation of Rs. 434 billion, which is a 3 per cent increase from the previous year, and for public security and Parliamentary affairs, a further Rs. 175 billion has been allocated. As far as I am concerned and certainly as far as the North and the East is concerned, Madam, the police behave very much on the instructions of the military and we do not differentiate between the two. So, I would like to include that figure with defence for my analysis.\n\nNow, defence, very clearly, has been prioritized over social welfare. I mean, the figures are quite staggering, Madam! Social welfare has been slashed, slashed, to Rs. 43 billion. That is, slashed by Rs. 35 billion compared to last year at a time when the most vulnerable sections of this country are struggling because of the economic crisis and the increasing prices. But, you all are slashing all the sectors which actually need to be enhanced; when the most vulnerable need to be protected and in some way strengthened, they are doing the opposite. In fact, the IMF recommendation was that social welfare must be enhanced and there was even criticism of the previous Government that there was not adequate support given to social welfare. So, this would terribly affect the very vulnerable sections, not just in the North and East - certainly, in the North and the East because if you take the GDP, the Northern Province is the last out of all the provinces - but in the entire country. So, we are right at the bottom and are affected very really by these slashes."
