    "md5": "f88b15d88f7dfabb00dea279c89631df",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "In fact, I personally went and had a look. At that time when I went and had a look, the police had not taken any action; the Magistrate had not been informed. It was one of our party members, who also happens to be in the committee of the cemetery, whom I asked to make a formal complaint to the police. Thereafter, the Magistrate got involved and the matter is now being looked into. But, with the question of mass graves, Hon. Minister, there is no protocol. To my knowledge, there is no clear protocol to handle mass graves. As a result, there is a severe shortage of funds and it is almost always the case where the Government Agent is asked to get involved. The OMP, I think, has a little bit of funds, but nowhere close to the sort of resources required for the work that has to be done by the Department of Government Analyst and the courts to have this matter looked into as a matter of urgency. As a result, because of things getting dragged on, both in Kokkuthoduvai in Mannar and now, in Chemmani, Jaffna, evidence can get destroyed or tampered with. These are questions of mass graves and\n\n501 502\n\n2025 \u0dcf\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8 01\n\nyou know that the major accusation with regard to the Chemmani case is that over 600 people - civilians - were killed and buried in that vicinity. Then, there were accusations during the Chandrika Bandaranaike's Government that overnight, there were excavations being done. So, the suspicion is that the bodies from the Chemmani mass graves had been removed and dumped in this cemetery. So, that is a suspicion. If you do not have the sort of resources primarily to protect that area and to ensure that the evidence is not lost, a grave injustice is likely to happen. So, that is another area that the Hon. Minister has to look into."
