    "md5": "f84210b975b97dee88bac9b9bfa552c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1746,
    "chunk_text": "you know that the major accusation with regard to the Chemmani case is that over 600 people - civilians - were killed and buried in that vicinity. Then, there were accusations during the Chandrika Bandaranaike's Government that overnight, there were excavations being done. So, the suspicion is that the bodies from the Chemmani mass graves had been removed and dumped in this cemetery. So, that is a suspicion. If you do not have the sort of resources primarily to protect that area and to ensure that the evidence is not lost, a grave injustice is likely to happen. So, that is another area that the Hon. Minister has to look into.\n\nSo, it is in that context I am raising these issues and as far as I am concerned, the evidence must be protected. You must, at least, try and protect that evidence. With regard to how these issues are to be looked at, you all have been suggesting an internal mechanism. My view is that that is just a non-starter. So, at the very least, if you could ensure that this evidence is protected and is recorded in a credible manner, I think that is the best you could do under these circumstances. I, for one, am not going to ask you to actually investigate it. I wish Sri Lanka was different; I wish we, as victims, could have said that we have credibility, that we look upon the system with credibility, but we do not. Nothing has happened so far to suggest that that situation will change. So, my request to you is that you intervene and ensure that adequate funds, adequate resources are given to protect the evidence in the mass graves so that at one point, when there is some agreement with regard to how to proceed on the question of accountability with the victims, then, this evidence could properly be used."
  },
