    "md5": "f4e786c85b2f335ff3c9b47f504d0c08",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1318,
    "chunk_text": "Madam Chairperson, William E Gladstone, a former British Prime Minister, in the late 1800s famously said, \u201cJustice delayed is justice denied\u201d. I think the Hon. Minister of Justice also mentioned that in his speech. That legal maxim means, if legal redress or equitable relief available to a litigant is not forthcoming, then it is same as there not being a remedy at all. If you look at the position in Sri Lanka, I think we all would agree that there are so many delays in our legal system, which has a\n\n507 508\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dc0\n\nhuge social impact, on the lives and livelihoods of our people. Sometimes, people have to wait for generations to get some sort of redress from the judicial system.\n\nThe Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration also mentioned in his opening speech about the delays in our legal system and as I mentioned before, this definitely has a huge social impact on the accused, the defendant and all the parties concerned. A person, until proven guilty, is deemed innocent. Most people accused of serious crimes often lose their jobs and would have to face hardships in their personal relationships. Also, the cost incurred by both the accused and the aggrieved party is immense. Therefore, like I said before, we have to take steps to hasten the process."
