    "chunk_index": 176,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "25781457313e3ab53bb7065f0ca476c7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1489,
    "chunk_text": "The Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration also mentioned in his opening speech about the delays in our legal system and as I mentioned before, this definitely has a huge social impact on the accused, the defendant and all the parties concerned. A person, until proven guilty, is deemed innocent. Most people accused of serious crimes often lose their jobs and would have to face hardships in their personal relationships. Also, the cost incurred by both the accused and the aggrieved party is immense. Therefore, like I said before, we have to take steps to hasten the process.\n\nSo, how can we speed up the process? I would like to make a few suggestions since the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration has just come back to the House. One suggestion is to create an administrative tribunal because, as we know, in the Supreme Court, there are a lot of fundamental right cases with regard to teacher transfers and school admissions, by which a significant amount of court time is consumed. Therefore, I would like to suggest an administrative tribunal be established so that these courts can be staffed by retired judges since we know that, in Sri Lanka, the retirement age for judges is 65 years. Then, definitely there would be another ten to fifteen years' time during which they could provide a service to our judicial system. Also, the cases can be taken up on a regional basis so that the number which come up to the Supreme Court could somewhat be minimized."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
