    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "954dae7f221c0812d64eb63e07c2f14f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1330,
    "chunk_text": "So, how can we speed up the process? I would like to make a few suggestions since the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration has just come back to the House. One suggestion is to create an administrative tribunal because, as we know, in the Supreme Court, there are a lot of fundamental right cases with regard to teacher transfers and school admissions, by which a significant amount of court time is consumed. Therefore, I would like to suggest an administrative tribunal be established so that these courts can be staffed by retired judges since we know that, in Sri Lanka, the retirement age for judges is 65 years. Then, definitely there would be another ten to fifteen years' time during which they could provide a service to our judicial system. Also, the cases can be taken up on a regional basis so that the number which come up to the Supreme Court could somewhat be minimized.\n\nAnother suggestion I would like to make is, empowering judges to impose realistic costs. As you know, at the moment, judges in our legal system do not seem to award high costs and as a result, we see a lot of frivolous actions being instituted against people. Therefore, if we have some sort of guidelines in place by updating the relevant gazettes, the judges could award higher costs, which would also help speed up the process."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
