    "chunk_text": "The independence of the Judiciary is a cornerstone of our democracy and it must remain untarnished and free from undue attack. In today\u2019s era of social media, attacks on judges made on the Floor of the House can have far-reaching consequences, eroding public confidence in the Judiciary. So, it is not only against a person, but also against the system. Judges, by virtue of their role, cannot respond to the allegations; you make statements against a person who is muted. I say that it is a cowardly act. I am not suggesting that Parliament should avoid addressing concerns regarding judges, they should. But, our Standing Orders should be amended to ensure that such matters are first examined through a structured process. Hon. Minister of justice, if you would look at the Standing Orders, you would notice that especially with regard to judges, there is a process to be followed before a Member comes to Parliament and makes various statements attacking them.\n\nAnother critical procedural issue that requires immediate attention is the lack of recorded reasons when leave or notice is not granted. Hon. Minister, you had been a practising lawyer before you took this high office."
