    "md5": "c9a1c14335291b3d239f0d389703429e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1721,
    "chunk_text": "Another critical procedural issue that requires immediate attention is the lack of recorded reasons when leave or notice is not granted. Hon. Minister, you had been a practising lawyer before you took this high office.\n\nMadam, another issue that requires urgent attention is the Supreme Court. It is overburdened with work. Several factors have contributed to that, the Supreme Court being the sole forum for hearing fundamental rights jurisdiction. That also has to be reconsidered, whether the Court of Appeal should be given that jurisdiction. Then, direct civil appeals from the provincial courts - earlier, those appeals go from the District Court to the Court of Appeal and then, to the Supreme Court - has further burdened the Supreme Court. Also, appeals from the Commercial High Court go directly to the Supreme Court. Then, there is transfer of writ jurisdiction to the Supreme Court under various laws. When the 1978 Constitution was introduced, the fundamental rights jurisdiction of the Supreme Court was limited and the Court focused on cases of public importance. However, today, on matters relating to the National Police Commission, the Public Service Commission and even the Monetary Board, if you want to file a writ, you have to file it in the Supreme Court. It has burdened the Supreme Court further. Sometimes, in relation to the Public Service Commission and the other commissions, the Supreme Court acts as a glorified labour tribunal because it adjudicates transfers, promotions and dismissals. So, the Court is burdened. Therefore, this heavy caseload results in severe delays and prevents the Supreme Court from dedicating its full attention to matters of significant legal and public interest."
  },
